    excerpt: "With fake internship listings common on Indian platforms, knowing how to identify genuine opportunities is essential. Here is how to find real internships and avoid scams.",
    cover_image: "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1531482615713-2afd69097998?w=800&q=80",
    category: "Internships",
    tags: ["Internships", "Freshers", "India", "Job Search", "Scam Awareness"],
    author: "Career Updates Team",
    status: "published",
    featured: false,
    seo_title: "How to Find Legitimate Internships in India | Career Updates",
    seo_description: "A guide to finding genuine internships in India and spotting fake or exploitative listings. Platforms, red flags, and questions to ask.",
    published_at: daysAgo(5),
    content: "# How to Find Legitimate Internships in India\n\nInternships are one of the most valuable experiences a student or fresher can have. But the Indian internship landscape includes a mix of genuine opportunities and exploitative or outright fake listings.\n\n## Where to Find Genuine Internships\n\n### 1. Internshala\nInternshala (internshala.com) is the largest dedicated internship platform in India. It has a verified employer system and a large catalogue of paid and unpaid internships across tech, design, marketing, law, and more.\n\n### 2. LinkedIn\nLinkedIn is increasingly used by startups and mid-size companies to post internship roles.\n\n### 3. Company Career Pages\nLarge companies in India — TCS, Infosys, Wipro, Cognizant, Amazon India, Google India, Razorpay, and many others — run formal internship programmes.\n\n### 4. College T&P Cell\nYour college's Training and Placement (T&P) cell coordinates with companies that recruit from your campus.\n\n### 5. Reach Out Directly\nSend a direct cold email to a company's HR team or a relevant lead. This takes more effort but can yield strong results, especially at startups.\n\n### 6. Referrals\nAsk seniors, professors, and alumni who are working in industry if their company takes interns.\n\n## How to Identify a Legitimate Internship\n\n**Positive signs:**\n- A clear job description with defined responsibilities\n- A named company with a verifiable online presence\n- Contact details of a real person with a corporate email\n- A defined duration, location, and stipend\n- An interview process before selection\n- Offer letter on company letterhead after selection\n\n## How to Spot Fake or Exploitative Internships\n\n**Warning signs:**\n- **Upfront payment required:** A legitimate company will never ask you to pay a registration fee, training fee, or security deposit.\n- **No interview:** If someone offers an internship without any screening, be sceptical.\n- **Vague job description:** 'Work from home, earn up to Rs.50,000/month, no experience needed.'\n- **Personal email addresses:** A recruiter contacting you from Gmail claiming to represent a large company is a red flag.\n- **Unsolicited WhatsApp messages:** Legitimate companies do not recruit this way.\n\n## What Questions to Ask Before Accepting\n\n- What will my day-to-day responsibilities be?\n- Who will I report to?\n- Is this internship paid? What is the stipend?\n- Will I receive a formal offer letter and completion certificate?\n- Is there a possibility of a PPO at the end?\n\n## What to Do if You Encounter a Scam\n\n- Do not pay any money\n- Report the listing on the platform where you found it\n- Report to the National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal: cybercrime.gov.in\n- Warn others on college groups and forums"
